UID:d2172e5250ea0fcb@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172346Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Standing Order: Standing Order Requiring Pape
 r Filing for Election Day 2026 Emergency Matters
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE       SUPERIOR COURT \nDISTRICT COURT \n \n  \n
 STANDING ORDER REQUIRING \nPAPER FILING FOR ELECTION DAY \n2026 EMERGENCY 
 MATTERS \n \n \n The Maine Judicial Branch (MJB) is implementing the Maine
  eCourts \nsystem – including eFiling – statewide.1  EFileMaine is a p
 ractical and efficient \ndocument submission system already in use in many
  courts throughout the \nstate.  However\, because filings related to Elec
 tion Day may be uniquely time-\nsensitive\, to ensure timely processing\, 
 the MJB will require paper filing under \nthe limited circumstances descri
 bed below.   \n \n It is therefore ORDERED that any emergency Title 21-A f
 ilings related to \nElection Day 2026 filed between 4:00 p.m. on Friday\, 
 October 30\, 2026\, and \n9:00 p.m. on Tuesday\, November 3\, 2026\, must 
 be by paper\, unless otherwise \nordered.2 \n \nIt is further ORDERED that
  the Maine Rules of Electronic Court Systems \nare SUSPENDED to the extent
  necessary to give effect to this order.   \n \n \n \nDated: 10/28/2026   
 _____/s/______________________   \n       Chief Justice Robert E. Mullen \
 n \n \n \nDated: 10/28/2026   ___/s/_______________________   \n       Chi
 ef Judge Brent Davis \n \n1 Further information about the Maine eCourts sy
 stem is available at \nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/ecourts/index.html \n2
  Any Election Day 2026 filings filed by eFile during this period may not b
 e processed until the next business \nday after the election.\nhttps://www
 .courts.maine.gov/adminorders/so-election-day-procedure.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/adminorders/so-election-day-procedure.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17826b6223eeac1c@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172323Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Seal Cove Auto Museum v. John J. Sanford et a
 l.
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
   2026 ME 110 \nDocket: BCD-25-555 \nArgued: October 8\, 2026 \nDecided:  
 November 19\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, DOU
 GLAS\, and LIPEZ\, JJ.  \n \n \nSEAL COVE AUTO MUSEUM \n \nv. \n \nJOHN J.
  SANFORD et al. \n \n \nLIPEZ\, J. \n[¶1]  In 2020\, the parties to this 
 appeal\, Seal Cove Auto Museum \, the \nRichard C. Paine\, Jr.\, Automobil
 e Collection Charitable Trust  (the Trust or the \nCharitable Trust)\, and
  the Trust’s trustees\, John J. Sanford and John  Peabody \nMonks Higgin
 s (the Trustees) \, entered into a settlement agreement and an \namended r
 elationship agreement to resolve a legal dispute.  In 2022\, after the \nT
 rust announced a plan to donate $1\,000\,000 to the Owls Head Transportati
 on \nMuseum\, Seal Cove filed a motion to enforce\, arguing that the donat
 ion to Owls \nHead violated the Trust’s and the Trustees’ obligations 
 under the agreements.  \nAfter a bench trial\, t he Business and Consumer 
 Docket ( Duddy\, J.) denied the \nmotion\, and Seal Cove now appeals\, pri
 marily asserting  that the trial court \nmisinterpreted relevant provision
 s of the amended relationship agreement and \n \n \n2 \nerred in finding t
 hat the Trust had not breached that agreement.  Upon review \nof Seal Cove
 ’s arguments\, we conclude that they lack merit\, and we accordingly \na
 ffirm. \nI.  BACKGROUND \n [¶2]  This is not the first time that we have 
 addressed an appeal involving \nthese parties.  As we explained in Attorne
 y General v. Sanford: \nRichard Paine Jr. established Seal Cove in 1963 an
 d the \nRichard C. Paine\, Jr.\, Automobile Collection Charitable Trust (t
 he \nTrust) in 1986.  The Trust and Seal Cove were established to \nmainta
 in and display Paine ’s large collection of antique \nautomobiles.  Foll
 owing Paine's death in 2007\, the Trust acquired \nmost of Paine ’s auto
 mobiles and an endowment to support \nmaintenance and display of the autom
 obile collection.  Sanford and \nHiggins presently serve as trustees of th
 e Trust. \n \n. . . The Declara\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/l
 awcourt/2026/26me110.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me110.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:da82bd7ef41841d4@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172323Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Jennifer L. Owen v. Town and Country Federal 
 Credit Union
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT      Reporter of Decisions \nDeci
 sion:  2026 ME 105 \nDocket:   BCD-23-442 \nArgued:   September 11\, 2024 
 \nDecided:  November 3\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, LA
 WRENCE\, DOUGLAS\, JJ.\, and HUMPHREY\, A.R.J. \n \n \nJENNIFER L. OWEN \n
  \nv. \n \nTOWN AND COUNTRY FEDERAL CREDIT UNION \n \nDOUGLAS\, J. \n[¶1]
   Jennifer L. Owen appeals from a judgment entered in the Business \nand C
 onsumer Docket (Duddy\, J.) after a jury trial in favor of Town and Countr
 y \nFederal Credit Union on her claims alleging disability discrimination 
 and \nretaliation.  Owen contends that the court abused its discretion by 
 (1) ordering \nher to pay attorney fees and costs as a discovery sanction\
 , and (2) excluding \nevidence of other former employees’ discrimination
  claims against Town and \nCountry.  We affirm the judgment. \n 2 \nI.  BA
 CKGROUND \nA. Owen’s Employment by Town and Country \n[¶2]  The followi
 ng facts are drawn from competent evidence admitted \nat trial\, viewed in
  the light most favorable to the jury’s verdict in favor of Town \nand C
 ountry.  See Sullivan v. Porter\, 2004 ME 134\, ¶ 12\, 861 A.2d 625.   \n
 [¶3]  Town and Country hired Owen as a teller in 2012.  The following \ny
 ear\, Owen was diagnosed with breast cancer.  Owen remained employed with 
 \nTown and Country and received support from her employer during her \ntre
 atment and recovery.  Town and Country and its employees organized a bake 
 \nsale\, a yard sale\, and other events to raise money to help her pay her
  medical \nexpenses.  Town and Country allowed other employees to donate p
 aid time off \nto Owen during her treatment.  By early 2014\, Owen was can
 cer-free.   \n[¶4]  In 2015\, Owen was promoted to the position of assist
 ant branch \nmanager in one of Town and Country’s South Portland branche
 s.  In this \nposition\, Owen was entrusted with managerial responsibiliti
 es in addition to \nworking with members on loans.   \n[¶5]  During the e
 nsuing year\, Owen’s performance in the assistant \nmanager position was
  unsatisfactory.  Al\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/202
 6/26me105.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me105.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d21b7508121aa2fa@role-business-corporate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261028T223326Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor recovers $603K for 829
  workers  denied overtime pay by Indiana auto parts manufacturer
DESCRIPTION:INDIANAPOLIS – The U.S. Department of Labor has recovered 
 $603\,953 in back wages for 829 workers after an investigation found an In
 dianapolis-area vehicle parts manufacturer failed to properly calculate ov
 ertime wages.Investigators with the department’s Wage and Hour Division 
 found that Toa (USA) LLC\, a major automotive supplier that serves top-tie
 r automotive companies\, did not include a non-discretionary attendance bo
 nus for hourly employees when calculating overtime during applicable workw
 eeks\, in violation of the overtime provisions of the Fair Labor Standards
  Act. “When employers pay commissions\, differentials\, certain bonuses
 \, stipends\, or other incentives\, they must include them in the calculat
 ion of overtime pay\,” said Wage and Hour Division District Director Jua
 n Herrera in Indianapolis. “We encourage employers\, especially those wh
 o offer various types of incentive pay\, to review our compliance assistan
 ce materials and contact us with any questions about overtime requirements
 .”Toa (USA) LLC\, with manufacturing facilities in Mooresville\, is the 
 primary body structure and suspension components supplier for Subaru of In
 diana Automotive and Toyota Motor Engineering & Manufacturing North Americ
 a.Employees and employers can contact the Wage and Hour Division with ques
 tions and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-free helpline\,
  866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Employers are encouraged to use the agency’s i
 ndustry-specific compliance assistance toolkits to learn about their res
 ponsibilities under the laws enforced by the division. The agency’s PAI
 D program offers employers an opportunity to self-report and resolve pote
 ntial minimum wage and overtime violations under the FLSA\, as well as cer
 tain potential violations under the Family and Medical Leave Act.Learn mor
 e about the Wage and Hour Division\, including a search tool that workers 
 can use if they believe they may be owed back wages collected by the divis
 ion. Download the agency’s free WorkWise Timesheet app f\nhttp://www.d
 ol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261028
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261028
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
